<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jagst</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jagst is a river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, about 190 km long and a right tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a>. It rises at the eastern edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-jura">Swabian Jura</a>, flows past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellwangen">Ellwangen</a> and Crailsheim through the Hohenlohe plain and joins the Neckar at Bad Friedrichshall. Its valley, with that of the parallel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kocher">Kocher</a>, formed the core of the Hohenlohe lands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jagst.jpg" alt="Jagst" width="320" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BerndH · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jagsttal_-_Br%C3%BCcke_bei_der_Heinzenm%C3%BChle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
